---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header, { navItems } from '../../components/Header.astro';
import MobileMenuOverlay from '../../components/MobileMenuOverlay.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const expansionRules = [
  { constraint: 'stringMaxLength(n)', cases: 'n-1 accept / n accept / n+1 reject' },
  { constraint: 'stringMinLength(n)', cases: 'n-1 reject / n accept / n+1 accept' },
  { constraint: 'stringExactLength(n)', cases: 'n-1 reject / n accept / n+1 reject' },
  { constraint: 'required', cases: 'absent / empty / whitespace only / present' },
  { constraint: 'numberMax(n, exclusive)', cases: 'n-1 accept / n depends on exclusive / n+1 reject' },
  { constraint: 'numberStep(n)', cases: 'multiple of n accept / non-multiple reject' },
  { constraint: 'stringEmail', cases: 'valid / no @ / no domain / no local part / double dot' },
  { constraint: 'oneOf([...])', cases: 'every allowed value + one disallowed' },
  { constraint: 'unique', cases: 'existing value reject / unused value accept' },
  { constraint: 'relativeDate', cases: 'boundary inside / boundary / boundary outside' },
  { constraint: 'conditionalRequired', cases: 'absent when condition holds reject / present accept / absent otherwise accept' },
  { constraint: 'conditionalOptional', cases: 'absent when condition holds accept / absent otherwise reject' },
  { constraint: 'fieldComparison', cases: 'satisfying side / equal boundary / violating side' },
  { constraint: 'stringPattern(re)', cases: 'the supplied valid and invalid examples' },
];

const corpus = [
  { name: 'Screen field table', blocks: 12, coverage: '100%', constraints: 29, unstructured: 0, rate: '100%' },
  { name: 'REST API spec', blocks: 9, coverage: '100%', constraints: 21, unstructured: 3, rate: '85.7%' },
  { name: 'REQ-xxx requirement list', blocks: 10, coverage: '100%', constraints: 13, unstructured: 1, rate: '92.3%' },
  { name: 'Narrative business spec', blocks: 5, coverage: '80%', constraints: 9, unstructured: 0, rate: '100%' },
  { name: 'Nested bullet list', blocks: 22, coverage: '100%', constraints: 23, unstructured: 1, rate: '95.7%' },
  { name: 'Batch job spec', blocks: 10, coverage: '100%', constraints: 23, unstructured: 6, rate: '73.9%' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="luq-spec"
  description="Extract constraints from a written specification with AI and expand them deterministically into a reviewable verification plan and a machine-readable IR."
>
  <Header />
  <MobileMenuOverlay navItems={navItems} />

  <main class="container mx-auto px-4 py-16 max-w-4xl">
    <a href="/tools" class="text-sm text-luq-teal-600 dark:text-luq-teal-400 hover:underline">← Verification Tools</a>

    <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-bold mb-4 font-mono text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">
      luq-spec
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      Turn a written specification into a verification plan and a machine-readable IR.
    </p>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">The problem</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A spec that says <em>at most 100 characters</em> implies exactly three tests:
          99, 100 and 101. That is not something to decide — it follows mechanically
          from the kind of constraint.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hand the whole job to a model and those three cases go missing differently
          every run. One time it drops the 101, the next time the 99. You can never say
          the suite is complete. So luq-spec splits the work: a model reads the prose,
          and a pure function does the expansion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Usage</h2>
      <CodeBlock
        language="bash"
        code={`# The plan a person reviews and approves
npx luq-spec plan spec.md --ai --out plan.md

# The IR, for diffing against constraints derived from the implementation
npx luq-spec ir spec.md --ai --out spec-ir.json`}
      />
      <p class="mt-4 text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        <code class="text-sm font-mono text-luq-purple-600 dark:text-luq-purple-400">--ai</code>
        drives extraction through a headless Claude Code run.
        <strong class="text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">No API key is involved</strong> —
        it reuses the authentication the <code class="text-sm font-mono">claude</code> command already has,
        so the library never receives or stores a credential. One call per document, because
        the harness cost is paid per invocation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">
        It stops at the IR — on purpose
      </h2>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        An earlier version emitted Playwright tests. It was removed. Binding a field name
        in a document to a selector on a screen is information the tool cannot derive, so
        most of what it produced did not run — and shipping that damages trust in the
        parts that do. What ships instead is a plan for a person and an IR for a machine.
      </p>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        The IR is the interesting half. Derive the same shape from the implementation —
        a DDL, framework validation declarations, or the observed behaviour of the running
        system — and the two can be diffed. A constraint present in the spec but absent
        from the implementation is a gap; the same constraint with different values is a
        contradiction. Neither is visible today without reading both by hand.
      </p>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        Neither output carries a timestamp, so an unchanged specification produces an
        identical file and a diff reads as exactly what changed.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        language="markdown"
        code={`## Customer registration / Company number

Spec: spec.md:14

| # | What to check | Input | Expected |
| ---: | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 12 characters is rejected | a x 12 | rejected (must be exactly 13) |
| 2 | 13 characters is accepted | a x 13 | accepted |
| 3 | 14 characters is rejected | a x 14 | rejected (must be exactly 13) |`}
      />
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Expansion rules</h2>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        This table is the whole expander. No inference enters here — one constraint always
        produces the same cases. Several rules deliberately emit both sides:
        <code class="text-sm font-mono">unique</code> checks that an existing value is rejected
        <em>and</em> that a fresh one is accepted, because testing one side alone passes an
        implementation that always rejects and one that always accepts.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-luq-neutral-300 dark:border-luq-neutral-700">
              <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Constraint</th>
              <th class="text-left py-3 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Cases produced</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400">
            {expansionRules.map((rule) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-luq-neutral-200 dark:border-luq-neutral-800">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs text-luq-purple-600 dark:text-luq-purple-400 whitespace-nowrap">{rule.constraint}</td>
                <td class="py-2">{rule.cases}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">
        Measured on a corpus, not one document
      </h2>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        Scoring 100% on a single specification proves nothing — it may just have been
        written to fit the extractor. These numbers come from six documents in
        deliberately different shapes.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-luq-neutral-300 dark:border-luq-neutral-700">
              <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Document shape</th>
              <th class="text-right py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Blocks</th>
              <th class="text-right py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Coverage</th>
              <th class="text-right py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Constraints</th>
              <th class="text-right py-3 font-semibold text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">Expanded</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 tabular-nums">
            {corpus.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-luq-neutral-200 dark:border-luq-neutral-800">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">{row.name}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">{row.blocks}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">{row.coverage}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">{row.constraints}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-right">{row.rate}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        Overall: coverage 98.2%, expansion rate 90.7%. Coverage counts blocks that yielded
        a constraint; the denominator excludes blocks classified as purely descriptive —
        and every excluded block is listed in the plan, because a metric you can quietly
        shrink is not a metric.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">
        Project-specific rules
      </h2>
      <p class="text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        What needs verifying differs per project — check digits, internal numbering
        schemes, bespoke formats. Those do not belong in a general library, so the
        expansion rules are extensible.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        code={`const checkDigit = defineConstraintExtension<{ numberKind: string }>({
  name: "checkDigit",
  guidance: "A field whose check digit must be validated.",
  // Required. A description alone is not enough — the model
  // falls back to "unstructured" without concrete phrasings.
  examplePhrases: ["The check digit is also validated."],
  parameterSchema: { type: "object", required: ["numberKind"], properties: { ... } },
  parseParameters: (value) => { /* validate untrusted model output */ },
  expand: ({ parameters }) => [ /* deterministic viewpoints */ ],
});`}
      />
      <p class="mt-4 text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        The extension receives only <em>identifying</em> information from the spec.
        Asking it to pull "a valid example number" would force the model to invent a
        value the document never contained. The check-digit algorithm lives in your
        code — that is where domain knowledge belongs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">
        What it refuses to do
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-luq-neutral-600 dark:text-luq-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          <strong class="text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">It does not invent examples from a regular expression.</strong>
          Valid and invalid samples must be supplied. Fabricated ones produce a test that
          passes while guaranteeing nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">It does not silently drop a line.</strong>
          Anything it cannot structure stays as <code class="text-sm font-mono">unstructured</code>
          and appears in the report.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-luq-neutral-900 dark:text-luq-neutral-100">It does not hide what it could not settle.</strong>
          Viewpoints whose expected result is undetermined stay in the plan, marked as
          needing a decision. Removing them would leave the impression that everything
          is covered.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
